<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE policymap [
<!ELEMENT policymap (policy)*>
<!ATTLIST policymap xmlns CDATA #FIXED ''>
<!ELEMENT policy EMPTY>
<!ATTLIST policy xmlns CDATA #FIXED '' domain NMTOKEN #REQUIRED
  name NMTOKEN #IMPLIED pattern CDATA #IMPLIED rights NMTOKEN #IMPLIED
  stealth NMTOKEN #IMPLIED value CDATA #IMPLIED>
]>
<!--
  LaraOCI hardened ImageMagick policy - spec section 3.2 / D13.

  !! NEVER put a backtick character anywhere in this file, including in these
  !! comments. ImageMagick's policy reader is a hand-rolled tokenizer, not an
  !! XML parser: it treats a backtick as a string delimiter, swallows the rest
  !! of the file, and loads an EMPTY policy - which means allow-everything.
  !! Nothing warns you. The build asserts the policy is live for this reason.

  Ghostscript is purged from the image, but the delegate and coder denials are
  what make that structural: they remove the entire ImageTragick class of
  attack, not just the one interpreter.

  domain="path" pattern="@*" is kept as defence in depth against the
  indirect-read syntax. Note that ImageMagick 7 does not route @-prefixed reads
  through the path policy - it fails them with "no decode delegate" instead - so
  this rule is unproven at runtime and must not be asserted as if it fired.

  MSVG and SVG are denied alongside MVG because they are the same vector engine
  under other names. Denying the external rsvg delegate does not close that
  path - it opens it: with no delegate available, an SVG read falls back to
  ImageMagick's INTERNAL renderer, which is the MVG code. Consumers who process
  user-supplied SVGs must do it outside Imagick.

  EPHEMERAL is denied and deliberately UNPROVEN, exactly like pattern="@*". On
  IM7 a read of it fails with "no decode delegate for this image format" before
  the coder policy is consulted, so no test can show this rule firing - and a
  test that appeared to would be passing for the wrong reason. INLINE and LABEL
  DO route through the coder policy and are asserted. Measured on 7.1.2.

  thread=1, with MAGICK_THREAD_LIMIT=1 in the environment, is a correctness fix
  as much as a security one: OpenMP pools are per process, so twenty FPM
  children each opening one will thrash a CPU-limited container. Consumers doing
  heavy batch work in the queue image can raise it.
-->
<policymap>
    <policy domain="delegate" rights="none" pattern="*"/>
    <policy domain="coder" rights="none" pattern="{PS,PS2,PS3,EPS,PDF,XPS}"/>
    <policy domain="coder" rights="none" pattern="{MSL,MVG,MSVG,SVG,MAGICK,TEXT,SHOW,WIN,PLT}"/>
    <policy domain="coder" rights="none" pattern="{EPHEMERAL,INLINE,LABEL}"/>
    <policy domain="coder" rights="none" pattern="{URL,HTTP,HTTPS,FTP}"/>
    <policy domain="path"  rights="none" pattern="@*"/>
    <policy domain="resource" name="memory" value="256MiB"/>
    <policy domain="resource" name="map"    value="512MiB"/>
    <policy domain="resource" name="width"  value="16KP"/>
    <policy domain="resource" name="height" value="16KP"/>
    <policy domain="resource" name="area"   value="128MP"/>
    <policy domain="resource" name="disk"   value="1GiB"/>
    <policy domain="resource" name="time"   value="60"/>
    <policy domain="resource" name="thread" value="1"/>
</policymap>
